6045417] [17592186167026 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167026 #edb/ref 1045 "Integration Using Basic Hyperbolic Identities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167026 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186167732 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167026 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048878 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167026 #edb/ref 1050 1380.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167026 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d2daf8ec-962f-5cd4-9714-2cfebccb518d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167400 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165608 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167400 #edb/ref 1069 "q-95742" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167400 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167400 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nEvaluate ${\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167400 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37030af8-6f60-5f87-96ef-c720c85ba743" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167400 #edb/ref 1074 "$$\nFirst, we find the hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nUsing Osborn's rule, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}.\n$$\n\nNow, we rewrite the integral using the above identity, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\frac{\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x}{\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x}\\cdot \\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\,{|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)-\\operatorname{cosh}⁡0 \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}+{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{0}+{e}^{-0}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{2+{2}^{-1}}{2}-\\frac{1+1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{4}-1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167464 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165992 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167464 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9628" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167464 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167464 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity ${cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167464 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "09b00f02-3f58-5541-80d9-f11db2b14004" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167464 #edb/ref 1074 "$$\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n{cos}^{2}⁡x+{sin}^{2}⁡x=1\n$$\n\nNotice that we have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n{cosh}^{2}⁡x\\,-\\,{sinh}^{2}⁡x=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167465 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186165998 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167465 #edb/ref 1069 "q-9631" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167465 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167465 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\nFind a hyperbolic identity that corresponds to the trigonometric identity $\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167465 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "3747ab66-800e-539f-851f-023604b67b37" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167465 #edb/ref 1074 "$$\n\nOsborn's rule allows us to construct a hyperbolic identity from a corresponding trigonometric identity.\n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of two trigonometric sines, flip the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nWe're given the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x\n$$\n\nNotice that we do not have a product of sines. Therefore, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sinh}⁡2x=2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167626 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167627 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167626 #edb/ref 1061 631340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167626 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "96a09359-527a-5e5b-b3ff-0751c279f524" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167626 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167627 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167627 #edb/ref 1061 631343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2b9ea8f0-27f6-52e0-95a8-a6c153a1617f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167628 #edb/ref 1061 631346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167628 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7dbc74d1-d41f-5d76-806c-c0b277105b07" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167628 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166793 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167732 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167732 #edb/ref 1061 631334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167732 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "4693d31f-c75b-551c-85f0-e6ea3040b97e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167732 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168117 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167761 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167803 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167761 #edb/ref 1061 631336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167761 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f36f0be-0a1c-5d94-9a98-49a786e635b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167761 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186166872 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167803 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167626 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167803 #edb/ref 1061 631339 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167803 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "02f143e3-76ee-5144-89d8-7142626764d4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167803 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168152 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167804 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167761 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167804 #edb/ref 1061 631335 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167804 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "25c4fda7-21cf-54ba-8f5a-d6ff1375cd9b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167804 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168153 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168117 #edb/ref 1064 "It's possible to convert every trigonometric identity to a corresponding *hyperbolic* identity using **Osborn's rule.** \n\nTo apply Osborn's rule, we follow two steps:\n\n1\\. Replace every trigonometric function with its corresponding hyperbolic function.\n\n2\\. For any product of *two trigonometric sines*, change the sign of the corresponding product of hyperbolic sines.\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the Pythagorean trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\cos^2 x \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\sin^2 x = 1\n$$\n\nNotice that this trigonometric identity contains a product of two sines. Therefore, to find the corresponding hyperbolic identity, we change each trigonometric function to its corresponding hyperbolic counterpart and change the sign in front of the corresponding product of sines. \n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n\\cosh^2 x \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\sinh^2 x = 1.\n$$\n\n \n\nWe can check that this identity is indeed true using the definitions of the hyperbolic sine and cosine:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{cosh}^{2}⁡x-{sinh}^{2}⁡x & ={(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}))}^{2}-{(\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}))}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}-{({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x}-({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}+2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}}-(\\cancel{{e}^{2x}}-2+\\cancel{{e}^{-2x}})) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =1\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168117 #edb/ref 1065 5552 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168117 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b6c4b399-c2b4-5fcb-a5b8-60bb6d23c29a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168117 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168152 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to calculate the integral\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n        \\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2 x}\\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we note the following hyperbolic identity:\n\n$$\n\\csch{x} = \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write our integral as\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}} \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\left(\\dfrac{1}{\\sinh{x}}\\right)^2 \\,\\textrm{d}x\n= \\int \\csch^2{x} \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can see that this is just a standard hyperbolic integral:\n\n$$\n\\int \\dfrac{1}{\\sinh^2{x}}\\,\\textrm{d}x = \\int \\csch^2{x}\\,\\textrm{d}x = -\\coth{x} + C\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168152 #edb/ref 1065 7913 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168152 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "f612c2c5-2457-59c4-899a-0847e3f278a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168152 #edb/ref 1067 "Integration Using Hyperbolic Identities" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168153 #edb/ref 1064 "When applying Osborn's rule, we must be aware that sometimes a product of two sines is \"hidden.\"\n\nTo demonstrate, consider the following trigonometric identity:\n\n$$\n\\newcommand{\\sech}{\\mathop{\\rm sech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsinh}{\\mathop{\\rm arsinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcosh}{\\mathop{\\rm arcosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\artanh}{\\mathop{\\rm artanh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arsech}{\\mathop{\\rm arsech}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcsch}{\\mathop{\\rm arcsch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\arcoth}{\\mathop{\\rm arcoth}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\cosh}{\\mathop{\\rm cosh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\sinh}{\\mathop{\\rm sinh}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\csch}{\\mathop{\\rm csch}\\nolimits}\n\\newcommand{\\coth}{\\mathop{\\rm coth}\\nolimits}\n1 \\:{\\color{blue}{\\boldsymbol +}}\\: \\tan^2 x = \\sec^2 x\n$$\n\nAt first glance, it may seem that there is no product of sines, and we do not need to change any signs in the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nHowever, recall that\n\n$$\n\\tan x = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}\n$$\n\nwhich implies that\n\n$$\n\\tan^2 x = \\dfrac{\\sin^2 x}{\\cos^2 x}.\n$$\n\nSo, there is a hidden product of sines within the $\\tan^2 x$ term, and we need to flip the sign in front of this term when writing down the corresponding hyperbolic identity.\n\nThus, the corresponding hyperbolic identity is\n\n$$\n1 \\:{\\color{red}{\\boldsymbol -}}\\: \\tanh^2 x = \\sech^2 x.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168153 #edb/ref 1065 7914 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168153 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0cd7ebe1-9de5-5914-a1e9-4b88c94e5a0e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168153 #edb/ref 1067 "Osborn’s Rule in Cases Where a Product of Sines Is Hidden" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536289 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{104}{255}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536289 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536289 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "036a64a0-313e-560e-a52e-dc6b8481224c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536290 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\operatorname{sech}⁡x+3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536290 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536290 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "037ba1f6-bab8-5644-a9f9-1d0997895160" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536293 #edb/ref 1080 "5\\operatorname{sech}⁡x-3\\operatorname{tanh}⁡x+C" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536293 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536293 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "05a68fca-8c3b-59b9-b009-99d530c3e80c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536299 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(x-y)=\\operatorname{cosh}⁡x\\operatorname{cosh}⁡y+\\operatorname{sinh}⁡x\\operatorname{sinh}⁡y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536299 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536299 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "08e07aea-260c-5921-8de8-d04c22d40c3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536301 #edb/ref 1080 "\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(x+y)={cosh}^{2}⁡x+{sinh}^{2}⁡y" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536301 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186536301 #edb